  "query_preview": "..\n\nToday the upper level ridge will be centered over the Four\nCorners region, with active easterly flow continuing in\nsoutheastern Arizona. Convective debris may linger through the\nmorning, especially west of Tucson, though there should be\nenough clearing sky to provide surface heating and \ndestabilization by this afternoon. Though there will be some \ndrying in areas near the New Mexico border, ample moisture will \nbe in place elsewhere. Surface dew points exceeding 50 degrees \nacross the region and 55-60 degrees in Pinal, Pima, and Santa \nCruz counties should produce MUCAPE values of 1000-2000 J/kg. \nThis instability combined with up to 20-25 kts of effective \nshear will be conducive to severe, organized thunderstorms again\nthis afternoon and evening. Scattered thunderstorms should \nbegin to develop in the early afternoon, especially across an \narea of low level convergence from Pinal through eastern Pima \nand Santa Cruz counties. Inverted-V soundings producing \n1500-1800 J/kg of DCAPE will bring another severe wind threat, \nalong with the threat of blowing dust. 00Z HREF severe wind \nprobabilities (greater than 50 kts) favor areas near and west of\nthe aforementioned convergence zone through central Pima county\nand north through the Phoenix metro, which lines up with the \n06Z Day 1 SPC Slight Risk outlook. Though storm motion should \npreclude a widespread flash flooding threat, heavy rainfall \nrates of 1 to 2 inches per hour are likely with any thunderstorm\nwith precipitable water values increasing above 1.5 inches near\nand west of Tucson. \n\nHealthy precipitable water values should remain in the area as \nmean flow continues out of the east, though shear should come \ndown a bit by Tuesday as wind speeds aloft decrease with the\nridge becoming further displaced to the northeast. Daily \nchances for showers and thunderstorms will continue through the \nweek with day to day variability in locations and severity. \nThough there is some discrepancy in timing, ensemble models \ncontinue to show signs of an easterly wave arriving mid to late \nin the week, which would enhance thunderstorm chances. This wave\nalong with the northern displacement of the ridge would bring \nhigh temperatures down below normal, with best chances of lower\nhigh temperatures currently falling Friday through the coming \nweekend.",
